# What pkg-config answered, read as text where it is text and told where it
# is not (defect 241, panel 189's R8; design.md §4.19). Its answer and its
# complaint come back as files under `build/`, and `read_file` failed one
# that is not UTF-8 as `not_text`, which `libraries.resolve_packages` took for
# no answer: the flags a package's `.pc` names were dropped and the build went
# on at exit 0, and a complaint that is not UTF-8 was told as nothing at all.
# Both are read here through the compiler's own read of a source
# (`module/reading.source_text`), which holds a file that is not UTF-8 with
# its bytes, so each such byte is written by its value, as clang writes one
# it will not show.
#
# Its own module because `cli/libraries.hero` stands at its ceiling.
#
# **`\r\n` is a line end in both, read so where the bytes enter** (defect
# 285), as `toolchain.run_clang` reads clang's: a `pkg-config` writing its
# lines that way, as one built for Windows does, left a return in the words.
# The complaint, trimmed of its last line end and split at the others, told
# its last line `<U+000D>`; the answer's last flag carried it, so `-lz\r\n` sent
# the linker after `z\r` (exit 2, *internal error: linking failed*) and
# `-DSEVEN=7\r\n` warned in every compile. A lone return stays a return, as
# `strings.without_returns` keeps it, written by its code where it is shown.

use module/reading
use not_text
use cli/process
use cli/files
use cli/shell_split
use cli/strings
use emit/ffi_build
use parse
use source

# The words pkg-config answered for `package`, or the package failure that
# stops the build. An answer that is not UTF-8 holds a flag no `str` carries,
# so it can be neither checked against the list nor handed to clang as one
# word, and it is never taken for no answer; one that cannot be read back is
# not taken for none either. The message opens with the package marker, which
# `emit/ffi_build.package_problem` turns into the author's `ffi_package`.
function answer(path: str, package: str) -> str?
    read = reading.source_text(path)

    if read.is_err()
        return fail(
            code: "failed",
            msg: "heroes-ffi-package `" + package + "` answered pkg-config's question, and its answer could not be read back from " + path
        )

    if read.must().faults.len() == 0
        return ok(strings.without_returns(read.must().text))
    return fail(
        code: "failed",
        msg: "heroes-ffi-package `" + package + "` answered with bytes that are not UTF-8, `" + one_line(not_text.bytes_written(read.must().text, faults: read.must().faults)) + "`, so its flags can be neither checked nor handed to clang\n  save the package's `.pc` file as UTF-8, or name the library directly with `link`"
    )

# What pkg-config said when it refused, each byte of it that is not UTF-8
# written by its value, `<0xE9>`; "" where it said nothing, or nothing could
# be read back.
function complaint(path: str) -> str
    read = reading.source_text(path)

    if read.is_err()
        return ""
    return strings.without_returns(not_text.bytes_written(read.must().text, faults: read.must().faults))

# What the author is told when `pkg-config` refused `package` (defect 292):
# the package not installed where `pkg-config` does not read its own `.pc`,
# and where it does, the package installed and `pkg-config`'s own words on
# what stands in the way, a package it requires or a version of one. Every
# refusal was *not installed* until then, above `pkg-config` saying a package
# the named one requires was not found. The message opens with the package
# marker (`emit/ffi_build.package_problem` reads it into `ffi_package`), and
# the not-installed sentence is word for word the one the net reads as a
# machine lacking the library (`tests/harness/absence.hero`).
function refused(package: str, complained: str) -> str
    said = "\n  pkg-config said: " + shell_split.shown(strings.trimmed(complained), keep_lines: true)

    if known(package)
        return "heroes-ffi-package `" + package + "` is installed, and pkg-config cannot give its flags" + said + "\n  install what pkg-config says it lacks, or name the library directly with `link`"
    return "heroes-ffi-package `" + package + "` is not installed on this machine" + said + "\n  install its development files, or name the library directly with `link`"

# What the author is told where `pkg-config` could not be started, so nothing
# could be asked about `package` (defect 249): the package's `ffi_package` at
# its string, the marker first, and the way out, where it was a bare line with
# no code, no place and no route.
#
# **What is said is the operating system's own reason** (defect 323), `why`, as
# `toolchain.run_clang` asks it of clang. 2 is no program of that name on the
# PATH (POSIX's `ENOENT`, Windows' `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND`), and only it is *not
# on this machine*, in the sentence the net reads as a machine without
# `pkg-config` (`tests/harness/absence.hero`'s `TOOL_OPEN`), word for word, so
# the Windows box, which has none, still skips the cases that ask it. Every
# other reason is the machine refusing to start one that may well be there, a
# file of mode 644 on the PATH answering 13 (`EACCES`) on this Mac: it was told
# *not on this machine* all the same, and is named by its number now.
function unasked(package: str, why: i64) -> str
    if why == 2
        return "heroes-ffi-package `" + package + "` cannot be looked up: pkg-config is not on this machine, and `package \"" + package + "\"` asks it where the library is\n  install pkg-config, or name the library directly with `link`"
    return "heroes-ffi-package `" + package + "` cannot be looked up: pkg-config could not be started, the operating system's own reason being " + why.to_str() + ", and `package \"" + package + "\"` asks it where the library is\n  the machine refused to start `pkg-config`, for the reason that number names (13 is a file it may not run, on Linux and macOS): make it a program the machine starts, or name the library directly with `link`"

# Whether `pkg-config` reads `package`'s own `.pc`, asked with no requirement
# followed: `--maximum-traverse-depth=1 --modversion` is answered at exit 0
# for a package whose `Requires:` names one the machine lacks, and at exit 1
# for one not found, by pkgconf 3.0.7 (this Mac) and 1.8.1 (Debian, the Linux
# arm64 image), measured 2026-10-04. Without the depth, 1.8.1 follows the
# requirements for `--modversion`, `--path` and `--variable`, and both follow
# them for `--exists`. A `pkg-config` that refuses the option answers no, so
# its refusal reads as every refusal did before. Its words are not read.
function known(package: str) -> bool
    ran = process.run(
        program: "pkg-config",
        words: ["pkg-config", "--maximum-traverse-depth=1", "--modversion", "--", package],
        out: process.discard(),
        err: process.discard()
    )
    return ran.started && ran.code == 0

# The answer on one line, its line ends dropped: pkg-config ends its answer
# with one, and the message quotes the words.
function one_line(text: str) -> str
    kept: [str] @ []

    for ch in text.chars()
        if ch != "\n" && ch != "\r"
            kept @ kept.push(ch)

    return kept.join("")

## Tests

# Defect 241's two answers, committed as the bytes pkg-config gave for a `.pc`
# whose words hold a Latin-1 `é`: its flags, and its complaint for a package
# required by name that is not there.
test "an answer that is not UTF-8 stops the build naming its bytes, and a complaint that is not is told with them"
    fixtures = "tests/golden/surface-fixtures/notext241/"

    match answer(path: fixtures + "answer-latin1.txt", package: "u")
        .ok _ => assert false
        .err e =>
            assert e.code == "failed"
            assert e.msg == "heroes-ffi-package `u` answered with bytes that are not UTF-8, `-DX=caf<0xE9> -I/nowhere`, so its flags can be neither checked nor handed to clang\n  save the package's `.pc` file as UTF-8, or name the library directly with `link`"

    said = complaint(fixtures + "complaint-latin1.txt")
    assert said == "Package caf<0xE9>x was not found in the pkg-config search path.\nPackage 'caf<0xE9>x', required by 'u3', not found\n"

    # Text is itself, and a file that is not there is no answer anyone gave.
    assert !write_file(path: "build/selftest-package-answer.txt", text: "-I/inc -lz\n").is_err()
    assert answer(path: "build/selftest-package-answer.txt", package: "z").must() == "-I/inc -lz\n"
    assert complaint("build/selftest-package-answer-none.txt") == ""
    assert answer(path: "build/selftest-package-answer-none.txt", package: "z").is_err()

# Defect 285: what a `pkg-config` writing `\r\n` says, as lane b9-harness's
# stand-in said it, and an answer of two flags so written. No return reaches
# a flag or a note; a lone one stays, as `strings.without_returns` keeps it.
test "pkg-config's lines ended by a return and a line end are read as lines (defect 285)"
    complaint_path = "build/selftest-package-crlf-complaint.txt"
    answer_path = "build/selftest-package-crlf-answer.txt"
    assert !write_file(path: complaint_path, text: "Package zz9 was not found in the pkg-config search path.\r\nPackage 'zz9' not found\r\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: answer_path, text: "-DSEVEN=7 -lz\r\n").is_err()
    said = complaint(complaint_path)
    assert said == "Package zz9 was not found in the pkg-config search path.\nPackage 'zz9' not found\n"
    told = refused(package: "heroes-package-no-machine-has-285", complained: said)
    assert told == "heroes-ffi-package `heroes-package-no-machine-has-285` is not installed on this machine\n  pkg-config said: Package zz9 was not found in the pkg-config search path.\nPackage 'zz9' not found\n  install its development files, or name the library directly with `link`"
    answered = answer(path: answer_path, package: "z").must()
    assert answered == "-DSEVEN=7 -lz\n"
    assert shell_split.words(package: "z", said: answered).must() == ["-DSEVEN=7", "-lz"]
    assert !write_file(path: complaint_path, text: "one\rtwo\r").is_err()
    assert complaint(complaint_path) == "one\rtwo\r"

# Defect 249: where `pkg-config` does not start, the refusal opens with the
# package marker, so `emit/ffi_build.package_problem` reads it into the
# group's `ffi_package` at its string, with the way out as its note; the bare
# line it was named no code, no place and no route.
test "a package asked where pkg-config does not start is told at its string, with the way out (defect 249)"
    text = "extern \"zlib.h\" package \"zlib\"\n    constant Z_OK: i64\n\nfunction main()\n    print(Z_OK)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "nopc.hero", text: text)
    head = "heroes-ffi-package `zlib` cannot be looked up: pkg-config is not on this machine, and `package \"zlib\"` asks it where the library is"
    told = unasked(package: "zlib", why: 2)
    assert told == head + "\n  install pkg-config, or name the library directly with `link`"
    d = ffi_build.package_problem(s, parsed.decls, stderr: told, line: head).must()
    assert d.code == "ffi_package"
    assert source.span_text(s, d.span) == "\"zlib\""
    assert d.message == "the package `zlib` cannot be looked up: pkg-config is not on this machine, and `package \"zlib\"` asks it where the library is"
    assert d.notes == ["install pkg-config, or name the library directly with `link`"]

# Defect 323, made to fire: a file of mode 644 started by its path, which the
# machine refuses, on Linux and macOS for 13 (`EACCES`), and the refusal that
# reason gives names it and says nothing of a machine without `pkg-config`.
# On Windows a mode does not keep a file from running and a name without
# `.exe` is looked for as one, so there the refusal follows whatever number
# the machine gave.
test "a pkg-config the machine refuses to start is told by its reason, never as not on this machine (defect 323)"
    dir = "build/selftest-package-323"
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    tool = dir + "/pkg-config"
    assert !write_file(path: tool, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 0.29.2\n").is_err()
    assert !files.set_mode(path: tool, mode: 420).is_err()
    ran = process.run(program: tool, words: [tool, "--version"], out: process.discard(), err: process.discard())
    assert !ran.started
    told = unasked(package: "zlib", why: ran.why)

    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        assert ran.why == 13
        assert told == "heroes-ffi-package `zlib` cannot be looked up: pkg-config could not be started, the operating system's own reason being 13, and `package \"zlib\"` asks it where the library is\n  the machine refused to start `pkg-config`, for the reason that number names (13 is a file it may not run, on Linux and macOS): make it a program the machine starts, or name the library directly with `link`"
        head = "heroes-ffi-package `zlib` cannot be looked up: pkg-config could not be started, the operating system's own reason being 13, and `package \"zlib\"` asks it where the library is"
        text = "extern \"zlib.h\" package \"zlib\"\n    constant Z_OK: i64\n\nfunction main()\n    print(Z_OK)\n"
        parsed = parse.parse(text)
        s = source.new_source(name: "refused.hero", text: text)
        d = ffi_build.package_problem(s, parsed.decls, stderr: told, line: head).must()
        assert d.code == "ffi_package"
        assert source.span_text(s, d.span) == "\"zlib\""
